being strong and being explosive are not  the same thing  a strong individual is someone who can  move something extremely heavy from  point a to point b  an explosive individual is somebody  capable of running fast  changing direction quickly and jumping  high into the air they can also deliver  snappy punches and throw things long  distances  they exert strength but they do so very  quickly  how do you become more explosive then  the first place many people will turn to  is plyometric training however there's a  lot of confusion surrounding what  plyometric training actually is and what  it isn't  there's even more confusion surrounding  how to use it effectively and when to  turn to other similar tools  so let's jump in jump  like in plyometric box jumps plyometric  exercises  utilize rapid bursts of power with a  minimal ground contact time  in order to utilize the stretch  shortening cycle  an example is a counter movement jump  here the athlete lowers themselves  rapidly eccentric phase  pauses extremely briefly amortization  phase  then springs up into the air to land on  a box or other raised platform  concentric phase the term plyometric is  a little nebulous and open to  interpretation however  the stretch shortening cycle means that  you're lengthening and then contracting  the working muscles in a very short time  frame  this is something we do intuitively all  the time such as when you wind up a  punch  this is often described as being a way  to tap into the stored energy that comes  from stretching the muscle  like an elastic band this is incorrect  however  in fact the power of the stretch  shortening cycle appears to come from  the time the muscle spends  in the active state this allows for more  cross bridges to form inside the muscles  thereby giving the more traction as it  were to then exert  force the lengthening phase therefore  acts more like a run-up  but spend too long in the stretch  position and you'll lose some of that  potential because you're  no longer active that's why it's harder  and not easier to jump out of a squat  unless you just dropped into it very  briefly the former is an isometric  concentric movement  the latter is a plyometric concentric  movement this also means that the ground  contact time is actually not as  important as the time the muscles spend  in the active state thus a counter  movement jump from the ground is still  plyometric  that said there is also benefits to  minimizing ground contact time in other  scenarios if you want to become  truly explosive enter the verkoshansky  depth jump and shock training the best  example is the verkoshansky depth jump  this involves jumping off a raised  platform around 42 inches for maximum  benefit  and then launching back off the ground  after less than 0.2 seconds  this collision engages the myotatic  stretch response  the sudden lengthening of the muscles  causes them to then rapidly contract in  response  and this in turn allows the athlete to  draw upon huge reserves of explosive  strength  this same collision is also seen in  sprinting  over time practicing this will increase  the athlete's eccentric strength  as well as training the central nervous  system to anticipate the myotactic  reflex  and thus increase firing rate in short  athletes get a lot more explosive  and in one study volleyball players were  shown to gain a 14  increase in jump height using a depth  jump program  by the way if you want to see all the  studies that support all this  then head over to the link in the  description down below and read the  written version of this post  to turn the explosive push up into an  example of shock training we would need  to jump on each repetition  while keeping the time on the ground to  0.2 seconds or below  all while properly absorbing the impact  to avoid injury  this could translate to even more  explosive punching and throwing  and have useful benefit for max bench  press  that said there are also some rebound  elements involved in most explosive  movements  this is where athletic properties such  as ankle stiffness become important  tendon hysteresis describes how pliable  a tendon is  where lower hysteresis equals less  pliability when running an athlete's  calf actually plays a somewhat minimal  role during plantar flexion  as a lot of energy is returned by the  stiffness of the tendon  thus it can make more sense to think of  the muscles in terms of muscle tendon  units  fascia the thin film wrap that covers  the muscles organs and more  which i mentioned in almost every single  video also has elastic properties and is  able to return energy to the ground  think of it like being coated in a thin  layer of flubber  one of the most crucial things to  understand when utilizing plyometrics  is inter-serial rest periods rest times  between sets  if you are programming plyometrics or  shock training then you should also  include sufficient rest time  this is not quite as straightforward as  some vocal individuals on the web would  have you believe however  the argument goes that in order to  trigger useful adaptations with  plyometrics  you need a long recovery period this  then allows the individual to exert 100  of their max effort during each rep when  performing plyometrics  it has been suggested that this should  be in the region of two to five minutes  of intercept rest however other studies  call into question just how strictly  we should adhere to this one study for  example found meaningful improvements in  athletes using 30  60 and 120 second rest periods there was  no significant difference between those  groups  that said going longer during  inter-serial rest periods  still makes a lot of sense and is  generally standard practice the argument  for longer rest periods is that if you  don't rest sufficiently  then you won't be able to call upon your  max effort when you perform subsequent  reps of jumps  that's the key here max effort jumping  as high as possible  is akin to performing your one rep max  you're giving a hundred percent  and this is what you need to do if you  want to increase jump height and rapid  force production  to jump higher you need to create the  demand for jumping higher  by regularly trying to jump as high as  possible thus we should also use a  similar approach to rest times  and number of repetitions as though we  were training with max weights  number of repetitions should be  somewhere in the region of four to five  maximum  if the aim is to increase jump height  for example and rest time should be long  enough  to ensure max effort the next go round  the number of repetitions is also  limited by the amount of time we can  spend performing a set of plyometrics  after all this is a form of train that  uses the phosphogen lactic acid or  atp creatine system for rapid energy  release  that means we can't maintain good effort  for much longer than 10 seconds  how many reps can you cram into that  time to answer that we must also  consider the intra serial rest time  in other words how long should you pause  between each repetition  we already know the answer if we're  talking about depth jumps and shock  training  0.2 seconds otherwise the intra-serial  rest will be determined by the nature of  the exercise itself  there is an unavoidable rest period in  counter-movement jumps for example  of course fatigue also increases the  likelihood of injury  this is particularly important in any  exercise that involves leaping off of  heights  and absorbing large amounts of shock  should the athletes slip or allow their  knee to collapse inwards it's game over  this is true in general when using  plyometrics and especially things like  box jumps  make sure that there are no valgus knees  and that you are properly absorbing the  shock as you land  but it's also important to realize that  training plyometrics at higher rep  ranges and with less recovery  is not pointless as some would have you  believe for one this trains the ability  to call upon explosive force  even in a fatigued state this of course  is how we are often required to be  explosive in sports and in life  we can also combine plyometrics with  strength training the problem with  strength training for increasing running  speed  is that the ground contact time for a  sprinter is around 90 milliseconds  this is not enough time to tap into max  strength and so the rate of force  development is more important  the rate at which the sprinter can  generate power the rate at which they  can recruit the necessary motor units  it's combining plyometrics with strength  training that could result in the  greatest rapid recruitment  of type 2x muscle fibers complex  training therefore does exactly that it  combines strength training  with a matched plyometric movement for  example you might perform bench press  followed by explosive push-ups or squats  followed by counter movement jumps  complex training appears to work via  post-activation potentiation  this means that the muscle fibers and  nerves are primed for action  this is the same reason you feel super  light jumping after you've been  squatting with a weighted vest  neurons and spinal nerves that  coordinate the movement don't return to  their previous resting potential  meaning a weaker signal is required to  reactivate them it's also postulated  that the actin and myosin within the  muscles  become more sensitive to calcium from  the sarcoplasmic reticulum  generally it is performed with reps  using roughly 85 percent of the one rep  maximum  followed by lighter plyometrics this is  an advanced  technique though and not necessary for  most people  plyometric training is not the only way  to increase explosiveness  take the kettlebell swing for example  swinging kettlebell is an example of  ballistic training  this is about acceleration and power but  without necessarily including the  stretch shortening component  does this still build explosiveness in a  word yes  any time you're required to generate  force against resistance  and the aim is velocity you are  developing power and explosiveness  more specifically you're working on rate  of force development  this means you're training yourself to  recruit muscle fibers quickly  which is what you need when operating in  nearly any athletic environment  even among power lifters aiming to move  the bar quickly during strength training  will recruit more type 2x muscle fibers  which will result in faster strength  gains in short it's as though you are  adding just a little bit extra weight  onto the bar this is also why we see  strength athletes  using compensatory acceleration here  they practice using lighter weights on  some days  but combine that with greater  acceleration to compensate  the west side barbell system refers to  this as the dynamic  effort method ideally you should train  using fifty to seventy percent  of your one rep maximum to increase  neural drive  giving you greater control over your  strength and allowing you to explode out  the bottom of a given movement  and that is why bar speed is such a  useful measure too coaches look at how  quickly an athlete is moving the weight  and adjust accordingly the problem is  that when the weight gets too heavy  we're not able to move with enough speed  the intent changes  we're now grinding through the movement  if your squat max is going up  but your vertical is staying the same  try the dynamic effort method  this is also why sprinters training with  the sled pull saw improvements in their  running speed until they upped the  weight  to beyond 75 of their body weight and  this is another very important point to  consider  relative strength the lighter you are  the lesser max strength you need in  order to move  explosively it doesn't matter how much  you lift it matters  how quickly you can lift your own body  weight what should be standing out to  you here  is that this is very much a mental game  it's about the intent with which you  perform the movement  if you aim to get through a set of  push-ups the result will be very  different than if you aim to perform  them as quickly and powerfully as  possible  it doesn't even matter if you move you  can increase rate of force development  using ballistic isometrics too  these involve pushing or pulling  explosively against an immovable object  as quickly as you can  so you might push against the wall and  suddenly give a burst of effort  this could be another effective tool for  contrast training too that would  minimize fatigue  if used correctly this is also why it  can be useful to train using some kind  of external cue  if you want to develop a true  explosiveness strength is useless in a  vacuum  so why not aim to jump on command get a  spotter to blow a whistle at random  intervals  you could even use two different tones  but only jump when you hear one of them  in order to learn both reaction and  action suppression  this is once again where training goes  from theoretical and detached  to genuinely useful and applicable  so that's a pretty comprehensive primer  on training for explosiveness  i hope you guys found this one useful  and interesting if you did please leave  a like and share around  that would help me out immensely  subscribe and hit the notification  button if you want to see more like this  if you want to learn more about  functional training that can have real  world benefits for athletes and everyone  else  then check out my newly published book  functional training and beyond  and thank you so much to all of you for  your amazing support since the launch of  the book  it's been doing great if you read it and  enjoyed it please consider leaving a  review on amazon  as that can really help me to get that  early momentum going and if you're  looking for a training program that can  improve speed strength mobility  brain function and more check out my  ebook and training program  super functional training that's  available in the link down below and  there's a discount on right now  while so many of us are still in  lockdown either way thank you so much  again for watching this one guys  and bye for now  [Music]  you

